====================================================================================================
REGIME: total norm fixed
====================================================================================================
  g rescaled to |grad E| = 1 at every M: the SAME problem, finer.
  G=3, T=200000, R=0.45, readout var=1.0, both at matched TOTAL shots.

      M      tr V6      tr PS   tr/M V6   tr/M PS   lmax V6  |corr| V6   PS/V6
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
      8    0.00023    0.00039  0.000029  0.000049   0.00004     0.0339    1.67
     16    0.00047    0.00156  0.000029  0.000097   0.00004     0.0334    3.30
     32    0.00094    0.00623  0.000029  0.000195   0.00004     0.0216    6.60
     64    0.00190    0.02490  0.000030  0.000389   0.00005     0.0188   13.13
    128    0.00378    0.09960  0.000030  0.000778   0.00006     0.0133   26.34

  fitted tr(Cov) ~ M^alpha :   V6 alpha = +1.006     parameter-shift alpha = +2.000

====================================================================================================
READING IT
====================================================================================================
  Parameter-shift splits T over 2MG circuits, so its per-component variance
  rises linearly in M and tr(Cov) rises as M^2. That is the baseline.

  V6's exponent is the result. If it is BELOW parameter-shift's, the
  one-shot-informs-all-M property is genuine information reuse. If the two
  match, V6 has moved variance rather than removed it and the circuit
  advantage is a billing advantage only.

  The two regimes are expected to DISAGREE, and that disagreement is the
  point: with per-component magnitude fixed the cross term |grad E|^2 grows
  as M and eats the amortisation, while at fixed total norm it does not.
  Any claim about V6 scaling therefore has to state which regime it means.
